// The running example from the Mover Logic paper (Figure "Motivating Example,
// Revisited"): an atomic add() on a lock-protected shared counter x, together
// with a client whose data invariant is even(x).
//
// Mover Logic verifies that add() is *atomic* (a single reducible sequence),
// so add() gets the precise, client-independent postcondition
//     x == \old(x) + n  &&  result == x
// while the client separately maintains its own even(x) invariant.

// Shared counter, protected by lock m: every access is a both-mover as long as
// the accessing thread holds m.
var int x  both-mover if m == tid;

// The lock m: an acquire (0 -> tid) is a right-mover; a release (tid -> 0) is a
// left-mover.  These are the classic mover specifications for a mutex.
lock m  write right-mover if \old(m) == 0 && m == tid
        write left-mover  if \old(m) == tid && m == 0;

// add(): atomically increase x by the thread-local argument n and return the
// new value in the thread-local result.  Declared atomic (default mover N).
// (Mover Logic omits frame conditions, so we state m == \old(m) explicitly to
//  record that add leaves the lock as it found it.)
atomic
requires true
ensures  x == \old(x) + n && result == x && m == \old(m)
add() {
  acquire(m);
  t = x;          // read of x     (both-mover: lock held)
  t = t + n;      // local compute (both-mover)
  x = t;          // write of x    (both-mover: lock held)
  result = t;     // local
  release(m);
}

// A client with the data invariant even(x).  Each thread repeatedly adds 2 and
// so preserves evenness.  The rely/guarantee summarise the *atomic* effect of
// add(), not its internal (temporarily odd) states.
// The rely/guarantee are two-store predicates: an interference step preserves
// evenness of x.  This is reflexive (even(x) ==> even(x)) and transitive, as
// required, and crucially summarises only the *atomic* effect of add().
relies     even(\old(x)) ==> even(x)
guarantees even(\old(x)) ==> even(x)
requires   even(x)
ensures    even(x)
client() {
  yield;
  n = 2;
  add();
  yield;
  n = 2;
  add();
  yield;
}

// The counter starts at 0 (even) with the lock free.
init x == 0 && m == 0;

// The initial state forks two client threads (each begins with a yield, as in
// the paper's non-preemptive formalisation).
thread { client(); }
thread { client(); }
